20fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-337996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0$ is {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-337996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=7,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-{x}^{3},$ which is different from zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-337996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home