ogether with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-131001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "150eef360fa6ca4370556dd37534d366c488edc736bb946f58aa913b35ef5b0b"} {"question" "q-131001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-131001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "q-131001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fa1041c8920fa9ae00c2a70d0ddbeafb4ca245ae19850ea3678670e61672839"} {"question" "e-6566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6566.json" "file_sha256" "b5a37b22782d16ea63998bb6d506138cf1ce6ed238e9adb8148b0010e5973e9b"} {"question" "e-6566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6566.json" "file_sha256" "b5a37b22782d16ea63998bb6d506138cf1ce6ed238e9adb8148b0010e5973e9b"} {"question" "e-6565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6565.json" "file_sha256" "01fc6ca1282d0d81627f87afd6bbbaaa6d0c016235bdd5bb8f6d06e4b634d31c"} {"question" "e-6565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6565.json" "file_sha256" "01fc6ca1282d0d81627f87afd6bbbaaa6d0c016235bdd5bb8f6d06e4b634d31c"} {"question" "e-6568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6568.json" "file_sha256" "04c4eedc6a39f321853110a95ece2adc681295751de61c51b7304a9990aac096"} {"question" "e-6568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6568.json" "file_sha256" "04c4eedc6a39f321853110a95ece2adc681295751de61c51b7304a9990aac096"} {"question" "e-6567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6567.json" "file_sha256" "504d238e2ecf4e89843b168a663f292052855b4eb62c6a949b32041830476a79"} {"question" "e-6567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6567.json" "file_sha256" "504d238e2ecf4e89843b168a663f292052855b4eb62c6a949b32041830476a79"}] "usage" {"q-337919" [] "q-64963" [] "e-6566" [] "q-65014" [] "e-6565" [] "q-337990" [] "e-6568" [] "e-6567" [] "q-65017" []} "basis" 1967 "retractions" [[17592186152488 :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\nThe equation $\\sin (x)y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y = 2$ is {{field-2}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + xy - 2x = 0$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186152489 :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous. The equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 3xy^{′} - y = 0$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} - y^{′} + y = e^{x}$ is {{field-2}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + x^{2}y + x = 0$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186152550 :question/problem "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = x^{3}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?"] [17592186152554 :question/problem "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = e^{x}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?"] [17592186152553 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $xy^{″} - 2y^{′} + y = x^{2} + \\sin x, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 1$\n2. $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 10$\n3. $x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} + 16y = 0, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(2) = 0$"] [17592186151886 :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n1. $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n2. $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n3. $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$"] [17592186151886 :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n$y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'$.\n\n- The equation\n$y''+y'=x \\cos x$\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0$, and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}$.\n- The equation\n$yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186151885 :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n1. $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n2. $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n3. $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$"] [17592186151885 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n$(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.\n- The equation\n$y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x$, which is different from zero.\n- Writing the equation\n$y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f$, we have\n$y'' + x^3 y = 8$.\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8$, which is different from zero."] [17592186151888 :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?"] [17592186151888 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0\n$$"] [17592186151887 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n2. $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(4)= 1$\n3. $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1)= 5$"] [17592186151887 :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y$, then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4$. So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186149789 17592186149790 17592186149792 17592186149793 17592186149795 17592186149796 17592186149798 17592186149799 17592186149801 17592186149802 17592186149804 17592186149805 17592186150211 17592186150212 17592186150213 17592186150214 17592186150215 17592186150229 17592186150230 17592186150231 17592186150232 17592186150233 17592186150235 17592186150236 17592186150237 17592186150238 17592186150239] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149788 :answer-field/id] [17592186149788 :answer-field/key] [17592186149788 :answer-field/type] [17592186149791 :answer-field/id] [17592186149791 :answer-field/key] [17592186149791 :answer-field/type] [17592186149794 :answer-field/id] [17592186149794 :answer-field/key] [17592186149794 :answer-field/type] [17592186149797 :answer-field/id] [17592186149797 :answer-field/key] [17592186149797 :answer-field/type] [17592186149800 :answer-field/id] [17592186149800 :answer-field/key] [17592186149800 :answer-field/type] [17592186149803 :answer-field/id] [17592186149803 :answer-field/key] [17592186149803 :answer-field/type] [17592186150210 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150210 :answer-field/id] [17592186150210 :answer-field/key] [17592186150210 :answer-field/type] [17592186150228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150228 :answer-field/id] [17592186150228 :answer-field/key] [17592186150228 :answer-field/type] [17592186150234 :an